    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nPower of a pump lifting mass $m$ to height $h$ in time $t$ is $P = \\frac{mgh}{t}$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nMass of water $m = 500\\text{ kg}$, height $h = 25\\text{ m}$, time $t = 60\\text{ s}$, $g = 10\\text{ m/s}^2$.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nP = \\frac{mgh}{t}\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Work done in lifting water: $W = 500 \\times 10 \\times 25 = 125000\\text{ J}$.\n2. Time taken: $t = 60\\text{ s}$.\n3. Output power: $P = \\frac{125000}{60} = 2083.3\\text{ W}$.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: 2083.3 W\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nAlways convert minutes to seconds when computing power in Watts!",
