    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nRebound velocity from a fixed barrier is $v = e u$. Loss in kinetic energy is $\\Delta K = \\frac{1}{2} m u^2 (1 - e^2)$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nMass $m = 1\\text{ kg}$, incident speed $u = 12\\text{ m/s}$, $e = 0.5$.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\n\\Delta K = \\frac{1}{2} m u^2 (1 - e^2)\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Initial KE: $K_i = 0.5 \\times 1 \\times (12)^2 = 72.00\\text{ J}$.\n2. Rebound speed: $v = 0.5 \\times 12 = 6.00\\text{ m/s}$.\n3. Final KE: $K_f = 0.5 \\times 1 \\times (6.00)^2 = 18.00\\text{ J}$.\n4. Loss of KE: $\\Delta K = K_i - K_f = 54.00\\text{ J}$.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: 54.00 J\n\n6. QUICK TIP:\nFractional loss of kinetic energy in normal bounce from a fixed wall is simply $(1 - e^2)$.",
